# 1. Native operation authority owns coordination

Date: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict
and implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D008,D009,D013,D014,D016,D019.

V1 already has deep domain/request modules, but GPUI callers still coordinate
slots, protected-key IO and ordered effects. React must not inherit that authority.

Choose a principal-bound Rust actor with dispatch(Intent) and observe(ViewScope).
Keep feature transitions domain-owned inside one initial core crate. Native
bootstrap injects adapters and creates operator/Harness handles; JSON cannot
select the caller principal. Finite domain instructions exclude arbitrary SDK
method strings and any Harness Apply capability.

The interface includes atomic admission/context retirement, per-source supersession,
separate read/review/write/stream domains, priority/deadline/rate-limit semantics,
bounded queues, bounded delivery-sequence deduplication with safe session rotation,
single-use settlement and truthful Remote outcome. No remote IO
or blocking vault call occurs while the authoritative transition is locked.
Admitted work runs on the host's one async runtime; native adapters execute effects.

React owns raw editor text/undo. Rust owns immutable captured drafts/reviews.
Review/Apply/Save/Send capture fresh input in their actual production handlers;
Apply compares fresh input with the stored reviewed operation and executes only
that stored operation. Invalid/lossy input, changed context/draft/resource or a
consumed review refuses dispatch. Never rely only on a debounced revision.
RequestApply uses a native operator confirmation; actual Apply completion is
private, single-use and revalidated after the modal editor/context lease.
Every scoped intent/observation carries native-issued context and principal
admission. Async vault completions are generation/lease-bound; obsolete writes
receive orphan cleanup rather than reactivating credentials. Explicit one-time
secret reveal/copy stays in a scoped transient native dialog, never snapshots.

Alternatives considered under the design-it-twice skill:

| Design | Depth/locality | Cost |
|---|---|---|
| Actor dispatch/observe | Callers learn one authority seam; context and settlement stay together | Finite unions and observation backpressure need discipline |
| Typed scoped handles | Strong domain methods and revoked scopes; flexible internal composition | More handles/methods and IPC registries for callers to coordinate |
| Task-oriented scan board | Trivial first scan workflow | Public task surface grows across all parity features |

Recommend the actor with domain-specific types and a small generated presentation
facade. Retain the alternatives' explicit scoped validation and effortless scan
usage internally. Do not combine all their public surfaces or add pass-through
modules merely to obtain two method names.

Two real execution adapters (SDK/scripted), storage adapters (Keychain/session/
fault-injectable) and clocks justify internal seams. The interface is the test
surface: switch/replace/cancel while queued/dispatched, old/duplicate reply,
fresh-input races, double Apply, denied Harness Apply, unknown outcome/no replay,
priority/deadline/rate-limit containment and observation resynchronization.

See [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md) for ownership/wire details.
No app code has been written and no native/SDK test is claimed by this ADR.
## Presentation and Academy

- **T08 Shared presentation:** web and desktop consumers build from the same
  reviewed shared source; themes/fonts/routes render correctly. General desktop
  screens mount no Academy gamification/account effects or web manager routes.
- **T09 Native UI:** actual Tauri command path, menus/shortcuts, keyboard-only
  workflow, focus restoration, VoiceOver labels/order, zoom/large text, themes,
  reduced motion, long/empty/error content, and window resizing. Mock IPC tests
  alone cannot establish these. macOS WebDriver availability must be verified;
  use supported native accessibility/automation or recorded manual steps.
- **T10 Academy identity:** device grant, pending/slow_down/expiry/cancel, refresh,
  revocation, wrong issuer/client/scope, account switch/sign-out, and stale replies.
  Token management stays Rust-owned. The learner account is independent of tenant.
- **T11 Learning authority:** client bundles contain no answer keys/private lab
  evaluation, privileged routes, or assessor tokens. Forged verdict/award/local
  exam clock cannot pass; the hosted backend decides access, grading, and awards.
  Signed-in denial differs from guest sign-in; sandbox never uses tenant secrets.
- **T12 Offline work:** download lease/revision/account access, encrypted draft
  save/restart, locked vault, sign-out/account switch, withdrawn content, duplicate
  submission IDs, changed draft versions, and reconnection. No deferred action
  replay. Valid local work remains usable without claiming offline grading.

## Workflows and Harness

- **T13 Collection semantics:** exact capability subsets, large collections,
  bounded pagination/truncation, search/filter/page resets, stale/partial cache,
  consistent Home/Inbox source facts, and no false complete/zero/absent claims.
- **T14 API Client documents:** edit/save/conflict while typing, invalid input,
  environment/secret separation, import/path traversal/size errors, multipart,
  cookies/redirect auth, cancellation, history/download, Git/profile adoption,
  and recovery from interrupted writes. Arbitrary workbench targets cannot borrow
  privileged application credentials through renderer-supplied references.
- **T15 Harness process:** actual native binary and bounded bootstrap/JSONL
  protocol, malformed/oversized frames, correlation/duplicates, queue pressure,
  crash/EOF/cancel, context change, graceful/forced cleanup, and missing/tampered
  resources. No PATH launcher fallback or plaintext rollout/history.
- **T16 Harness authority:** typed investigation/proposal tools, active-draft
  protection, no engine Apply, one-time secrets absent from model context,
| Model/skill read-only observations     | C13             | Accepted actual reads and partial-source disclosure                                  |
| API Client documents/projects/imports  | C14             | Fresh-input reconciliation, drafts/conflicts/secrets/Git adoption                    |
| API Client transport/history/catalogue | C15             | Real HTTP/cookies/downloads, scoped auth, no mutation bypass                         |
| Gateway workspace/config/admin subset  | C16,C17         | Supported scope/options and reviewed multi-step outcomes                             |
| Gateway inference/logs/telemetry/cost  | C18             | Streaming, route/model selection, operational budgets/limits                         |
| Harness native run/stream/lifecycle    | C19             | Real Rust engine/protocol, cleanup and no packaged Node runtime                      |
| Harness identity/protected history     | C20             | Rust SSO/key lifecycle, scope-safe restore/export/delete                             |
| Harness typed tools/project permission | C21             | Investigation/proposals and enforced existing sandbox restrictions                   |
| Workspace MCP                          | C22             | Rust auth/grants, exact call approvals and execution evidence                        |
| Academy identity/account isolation     | C04             | Device OAuth, scope admission, tenant-independent learner identity                   |
| Academy learner presentation           | C05             | Shared catalog/lessons/checks/sandbox/UI; feature-local gamification                 |
| Academy Program/exams/certificates     | C06             | Hosted access/timing/grading/award authority and account workflows                   |
| Academy encrypted offline work         | C07             | Lease/revision/account-aware reading/drafts; explicit submission                     |
| Reviewed imports/fresh authentication  | C23             | User work, source preservation, restart/rollback; no old approvals                   |
| Native UX/privacy/measured performance | C01–C24         | Per-chunk checks plus cumulative actual native quality closure                       |
| Controlled live behavior               | C25             | Designated scope, real outcomes, readback/cleanup, honest gaps                       |
| Signed installed package/update        | C26             | DMG/notary/Gatekeeper, exact bytes, fresh user, coexistence/rollback                 |

## Required per-workflow ledger

The [workflow ledger](../architecture/workflows.md) expands these families into
66 operation/workflow classes. C01 records machine-readable evidence rows and
consuming chunks map exact supported operations/options before implementation.

## Intentional non-goals and retained classifications

- Financial desktop billing/monetization and media-generation infrastructure are
  excluded. Remote operational cost/limit administration is retained.
- Exception Patterns remains explicitly synthetic/inert if shown. FP/FN reports
  do not suppress scans or modify policy.
- New Red Team execution and dedicated Model Security execution remain outside
  parity. Their existence in the new SDK does not authorize adding product scope.
- Academy admin/assessor operations remain web-only. Learner feedback with an
  administrative namespace is classified by actual authority, not string matching.
- No browser app, in-app updater, or additional supported OS/architecture is
  required for first stable v2.
- Shared Academy appearance replaces the GPUI presentation implementation. Do
  not infer a pixel clone or identical internal widget/theme catalogue. Preserve
  theme preference workflows and specify reviewed mappings for imported settings.
- The generic SCM workbench catalogue does not prove all 193 requests were live
  tested. Classify its supported execution/admission separately from dedicated
  workflows and bind its request metadata to a reviewed source snapshot.

